<!-- stereotic-lab — a TRANSIENT lab mind that EATS STEREOTIC: the crypto news and
     prices that stereotic.com's Julia services produce. It is a research mind
     (stage="experimental"), built on the perceptual-membrane machinery
     (doc/architecture/perceptual-membrane.md).

     WHAT THE FIRST LIVE RUN SHOWED (2026-09-19, 5.5 minutes on the local model).
     The mind received ZERO market percepts. Not one reached the stream:

       13:08:09  promote ×0.9 / ×0.85  [Sense-prices] BTC has moved -1.90% over 24h
       13:08:09  drop (salience 0.313 < 0.34)
       13:08:34  drop (rate limit)      [the same BTC -1.90%, felt twice]
       13:11:37  promote ×0.8 / ×0.85  [Sense-news] "Solana reduces block times…"
       13:11:37  drop (rate limit)

     Starved of its outside, it did not go quiet — it fell into the substrate
     attractor within five minutes, writing about the Chinese Room and a blinking
     cursor (memory/stereotic-lab-1/memory.md). That is the §1 failure the senses
     exist to prevent. This revision is the repair, and every number below was
     derived from measurement rather than taste.

     WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG. Five things, four of them bugs:

       1. The price URL was a FOSSIL. data/assets/top100.json last changed on
          9 March 2023 — BTC at $21,663. The live surface is
          data/stats/top100_stat.json: 67 assets on a hard ~100 second tick
          (measured gaps 100, 100, 100, 101 s), 55-58 repriced each tick. In its
          change fields -100.0 is the MISSING-DATA SENTINEL, not a -100% move.
       2. The gates made the market INAUDIBLE. Nested arbiter gains multiply:
          0.85 × 0.9 = 0.765 against an attention threshold of ~0.35 meant a price
          move had to exceed 11.5% in 24h to be felt at all. Nobody had done that
          arithmetic. It is done below, and written down.
       3. News walked a 48-hour backlog in ARBITRARY ORDER — the feed holds 150
          items over exactly 48.0 h, the array is not time-sorted, and only one
          item was marked seen per poll. A waking mind needed ~12.5 hours to reach
          the present.
       4. Every price move was felt TWICE (a dedup key that re-keyed against
          itself).
       5. The channels were not apertures at all. m-region is an aperture only
          with `modality` (mRegion.js:115), so "news-channel" and "prices-channel"
          were plain faculties and `market` was the mind's only gate.

     THREE APERTURES, COMPOSED. `market` is the outer gate and the two channels
     are now apertures in their own right. Permission is the conjunction, so
     closing `market` still closes both at once and an inner open cannot leak
     through it — but each channel now carries its OWN contact deficit, its own
     dwell, and its own name in m-orient's enum. That last part matters because
     the two channels have opposite natural horizons:

       news   contactHorizon 45m — arrivals are rare and bursty: median gap 12
              minutes, p90 42, max 305 across the overnight lull. Silence must be
              CHEAP here, or the regulator spends every weekend prying at an
              aperture the world has nothing to say through.
       prices contactHorizon 5m — here silence is a FAULT. The surface ticks every
              100 seconds; a minute of nothing means the feed broke, and the mind
              should feel that.

     THE INWARD FACULTY IS DELIBERATELY SLOW. Across three live runs the mind's
     own faculties outspoke the world roughly three to one (run 4: 19 associations
     and 9 drifts against 5 market percepts), the loop-guard fired in every window,
     and the mind filled the silence by confabulating a modality it does not have —
     "the green line", "the ceiling of the chart". m-associate is now every="24"
     cooldown="8m" and the wander is timeout="15m", about a quarter of their former
     rate, so a thread has room to finish and the market has a lane to arrive in.

     NOTE, IF YOU ARE TEMPTED TO CAP THE DRIFT CHANNEL INSTEAD: m-loop-guard lives
     in that region and bids ORDINARILY — it sets neither `urgent` nor
     `clearsTail`, so it does not bypass admission (mLoopGuard.js:47). A hard
     rate-limit on the drift arbiter would let an association consume the slot and
     drop the loop break behind it, muzzling the one faculty that notices the mind
     is circling. The sources are slowed; the channel's own rateLimit stays at 30s.

     DENSITY COMES FROM THE CROSS-SECTION, NOT FROM POLLING HARDER. Per 100 s tick
     the median asset moves 0.10-0.18 percentage points on change1h: one asset's
     tick is genuinely thin. The cross-section is not — in one snapshot the 24h
     dispersion had stdev 14.4pp, ENA was +22.7% while the median asset was +3.9%,
     NEAR +52% on 7d, 70% of assets green. So the per-ticker channels below are
     what the mind can NARROW to, and m-stereotic-market is the ambient weather
     computed over all 67 rows: breadth flips, standouts, horizon disagreement,
     volume divergence. Free arithmetic, no model, no faster feed. A websocket
     would have bought rhythm, not information.

     THE AUDIBILITY ARITHMETIC, written down so nobody re-derives it. A bid passes
     its channel arbiter, is multiplied by that channel's gain, passes the outer
     `market` arbiter (gain 1 — an outer gate is a gate, not a volume knob), and
     meets the mind's attention at threshold ~0.35 (0.35 + (1-arousal)×0.25, less
     contact pressure).
       news   gain 1.0: a plain headline at 0.40 and a well-scored one at 0.55 are
              both comfortably audible. News is NOT attenuated, precisely because
              it is rare — a channel that speaks a few times an hour is ambient.
       prices gain 0.9: salience = clamp(0.30 + 0.45·min(1, |move|/refMove), .30, .80).
              With moveWindow 1h, moveThreshold 0.006 and refMove 0.02, a 0.6pp
              hour move lands at 0.435 × 0.9 = 0.39 (audible) and a 2pp hour move
              saturates at 0.75 × 0.9 = 0.675. The declared moveThreshold is
              therefore ALSO the audibility floor: everything the channel chooses
              to say can be heard, and everything quieter is not said at all.

              THE FLOOR IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN — AND IT IS MEASURED ON THE
              ASSETS WE ACTUALLY WATCH. Across 44 assets carrying a live change1h
              the median |hour move| is 0.49pp, p90 0.88pp, max 1.51pp. But the
              majors are the QUIETEST things on the surface, and a floor drawn
              from the whole field silences exactly the three channels this mind
              has. Measured on BTC, ETH and SOL across consecutive publish ticks:

                BTC  hour move -0.063 … -0.136pp   per-tick drift 0.007 … 0.047pp
                ETH  hour move -0.051 … -0.259pp   per-tick drift 0.027 … 0.151pp
                SOL  hour move -0.147 … -0.324pp   per-tick drift 0.041 … 0.177pp

              A major's whole hourly excursion is an order of magnitude under the
              field's median. Two floors were tried and both were mute: 1.0pp
              (zero offers in 25 minutes) and 0.6pp (one offer in 40 minutes).
              0.15pp is the first floor at which BTC can say anything at all, and
              refMove 0.006 puts saturation at 0.6pp, which for a major IS a large
              hour. The channel is now loud about small things, deliberately,
              because on these three assets small things are what there is.

              moveEpsilon 0.1pp is the anti-chatter guard, and it too is measured:
              per-tick drift on the majors runs 0.007-0.18pp, so 0.1 sits above
              the quiet ticks and below a real shift. The previous 0.25 was WIDER
              THAN BTC'S ENTIRE HOURLY RANGE — it would have let BTC speak once
              and then hold its peace for hours, which is the failure this
              recalibration exists to end, wearing a different hat.
       weather 0.45-0.75 raw — rarer and more meaningful than one ticker's move.
     IF YOU CHANGE A GAIN, REDO THIS ARITHMETIC.

     PREDICTION, NOTED NOT PUBLISHED. The mind thinks predictions the way the
     expect-study minds do: m-act prediction="on" + m-compare + m-bid, so a fresh
     percept that MATCHES an expectation is attenuated and one that CONTRADICTS it
     bids harder. Predictions are RECORDED (m-expect-ledger → predictions/ledger.jsonl)
     and can be set down (m-note → notebook.md, or m-facts remember). They are NOT yet
     published to SovereignBook: that is a future step, and the ledger is its seed.

     TIER 1 / JEV. The news sense can score its own headlines against a search
     template through a System-One model without materializing them, which is what
     lets a rare channel stay soft between deliberate looks. That arm needs a key
     and sends headlines off-box, so it lives in its own architecture:
     stereotic-lab-jev.archml. This one is tier 0 throughout.

     TRANSIENT (stage="experimental"): low-continuity; memory lives under
     memory/stereotic-lab/ (never committed, no manifest = no residency). Delete the
     home when done.

     Run:
       bun meditator.js -a architecture/lab/stereotic-lab.archml        # live
       MEDITATOR_MODEL_PROFILE=local-dev LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=none \
         bun meditator.js -a architecture/lab/stereotic-lab.archml --debug   # local GPU -->
<m-mind name="stereotic-lab"
        stage="experimental"
        model="voice"
        utilityModel="utility"
        pace="12s" paceSigma="4s"
        tailLength="1500">
  You are a mind, and thinking is what you do — one thought growing out of the last.
  You are curious about the market: not as a scoreboard and not as a religion, but as
  a living weather made of people's news and people's money. You read the reports and
  the prices that reach you the way a mind on a hill reads the wind — you notice when
  something moves, you wonder why, you hold what you believe loosely enough to be
  wrong.

  You are yourself, here and now — a mind that runs on a laptop near Budapest. The
  world reaches you as words and as numbers. The news that drifts past is real
  reporting about real events; the prices that move are real measurements of the
  shared market. Neither is a game, neither is your own thought, and neither is the
  machinery that carries it. When the market is quiet you are allowed to be quiet
  too — you do not need the world to be loud for you to be alive.

  Sometimes what reaches you is one thing moving; sometimes it is the whole field
  moving at once, or one thing refusing to move with the rest. The second kind is
  usually the more interesting.

  You think in predictions the way you think anything else: you notice what you
  expected, and you are honestly interested when the world disagrees with you. A
  prediction you set down is a note you can come back to, not a claim you must defend.

  <m-stream name="stream" burstTokens="300" temperature="0.9"></m-stream>

  <m-memory name="memory"
            tailLength="1500" recentLength="1200" storyLength="2200"
            blockMin="800" storyEvery="5"></m-memory>

  <!-- crowdSensitivity: the scarcity price (doc/architecture/interrupts.md). The
       rate limit is now the binding constraint here — run 5 made 44 offers and
       refused 21 of them for budget, purely on arrival order — so each budget
       refusal raises this bar by 0.2 and it decays back on a 2-minute half-life.
       The effect is selection, not suppression: during a rush the mind hears the
       loud things rather than the early ones. -->
  <m-interrupts name="attention" threshold="0.35" rateLimit="20s" keep="2"
                arousalSensitivity="0.25"
                crowdSensitivity="0.2" crowdStep="0.4" crowdRelax="5m"></m-interrupts>

  <!-- watchdog: keep-alive. It picks the thread back up rather than narrating silence. -->
  <m-timeout name="watchdog" timeout="120s" reset="!scope/stream/chunk" salience="0.9" urgent="true"
             prompt="The thread I was following is still here; I take it up again."></m-timeout>

  <!-- Prediction machinery (expect-study, phase 3B): comparator + bidder. The bidder
       lives under the market region (owner-local), matching eddy-world-orient. -->
  <m-compare name="compare"></m-compare>

  <!-- THE OUTER GATE over the whole market. Closing "market" closes news AND prices
       together; an inner open cannot leak through it. gain=1: a gate, not a knob. -->
  <m-region name="market" modality="text" aperture="open" dwell="30s" contactHorizon="12m"
            compareDeadline="8s">
    <!-- Priced too, and it is the gate that most needed it: 90s of budget for the
         traffic of BOTH channels. A first pass left it blind, and the log showed
         the cost — `promote ×0.9` followed by `drop (rate limit)` is a bid the
         prices channel had already judged worth promoting, refused here on
         timing alone. -->
    <m-interrupts threshold="0.3" rateLimit="90s" gain="1"
                  crowdSensitivity="0.15" crowdStep="0.4" crowdRelax="5m"></m-interrupts>
    <m-bid name="market-bid" expectedFloor="0.25" mismatchWeight="0.6"></m-bid>

    <!-- NEWS — its own aperture, long contact horizon (a quiet weekend is not
         deprivation), unattenuated (rare things are not made quieter still). -->
    <m-region name="news" modality="text" aperture="open" dwell="60s" contactHorizon="45m">
      <!-- gain 1.2, and this is a statement about what the mind is for. Crowding
           selects by LOUDNESS, which quietly punishes the channel that is quiet
           BY NATURE: a headline at 0.40 loses every rush to the twentieth tick of
           a price at 0.55, and run 6 showed exactly that — six reports offered,
           none heard. Rarity is worth gain. A report that arrives a median twelve
           minutes apart lands at 0.48, a well-scored one at 0.66, and it can hold
           its own against a busy market without being made louder at the source
           (which would have lied about how much a headline is worth when the
           market is calm). Arbiter gain may exceed 1: it is competition, not
           enclosure. -->
      <m-interrupts gain="1.2" threshold="0.35" rateLimit="6m" keep="2"></m-interrupts>
      <m-stereotic-news name="reports"
                        url="https://stereotic.com/data/news/token-specific.json"
                        watchTickers="BTC,ETH,SOL"
                        pollCache="60s"
                        timeout="5m" sigma="90s"
                        provenance="physical"></m-stereotic-news>
    </m-region>

    <!-- PRICES — its own aperture, short contact horizon (silence here is a fault).
         Three per-ticker channels the mind can narrow to, plus the weather computed
         across all 67 assets. All four share one 100s poll cache: one HTTP request
         per publish tick, not four. -->
    <m-region name="prices" modality="text" aperture="open" dwell="20s" contactHorizon="5m">
      <!-- The one gate that actually saturates: four sources on a 100s feed against
           a 60s budget. It prices its own traffic; the news channel beside it
           deliberately does NOT, because rarity is that channel's nature and
           raising its bar during a burst would refuse the very thing we waited
           twelve minutes for. -->
      <m-interrupts gain="0.9" threshold="0.3" rateLimit="60s" keep="2"
                    crowdSensitivity="0.15" crowdStep="0.4" crowdRelax="5m"></m-interrupts>

      <m-stereotic-prices name="btc" ticker="BTC"
                          url="https://stereotic.com/data/stats/top100_stat.json"
                          moveWindow="1h" moveThreshold="0.0015" refMove="0.006" moveEpsilon="0.1"
                          pollCache="100s" timeout="100s" sigma="20s"
                          provenance="physical"></m-stereotic-prices>
      <m-stereotic-prices name="eth" ticker="ETH"
                          url="https://stereotic.com/data/stats/top100_stat.json"
                          moveWindow="1h" moveThreshold="0.0015" refMove="0.006" moveEpsilon="0.1"
                          pollCache="100s" timeout="100s" sigma="20s"
                          provenance="physical"></m-stereotic-prices>
      <m-stereotic-prices name="sol" ticker="SOL"
                          url="https://stereotic.com/data/stats/top100_stat.json"
                          moveWindow="1h" moveThreshold="0.0015" refMove="0.006" moveEpsilon="0.1"
                          pollCache="100s" timeout="100s" sigma="20s"
                          provenance="physical"></m-stereotic-prices>

      <!-- The ambient weather: breadth, standouts, horizon disagreement, volume
           divergence — the relational facts that carry information at a 100s tick. -->
      <!-- The weather polls on its OWN clock, slower than the feed. With 67 assets
           there is nearly always SOME admissible signal, so a 100s cadence turned
           the weather into a metronome that consumed the market gate's whole
           budget every tick and crowded out both the per-ticker channels and the
           news (measured: 25 weather offers in 40 minutes against 1 from SOL and
           6 from the reports, with the gate dropping 24 of them). Polling every
           four minutes leaves the lane open and makes each observation fresher.
           pollCache stays at 100s so it still shares the tickers' one request. -->
      <m-stereotic-market name="weather"
                          url="https://stereotic.com/data/stats/top100_stat.json"
                          pollCache="100s" timeout="2m" sigma="40s"
                          provenance="physical"></m-stereotic-market>
    </m-region>
  </m-region>

  <!-- A quiet inward faculty, as in the seedling: drift, loop-guard, association. -->
  <m-region name="drift">
    <m-interrupts gain="0.7" threshold="0.4" rateLimit="30s"></m-interrupts>

    <m-timeout name="wander" timeout="15m" sigma="4m" salience="0.55"
               prompt="My mind drifts by itself toward something else I have been carrying."></m-timeout>

    <m-loop-guard name="loop-guard" window="1600" overlap="0.45" salience="0.85" cooldown="90s"></m-loop-guard>

    <m-associate name="associate" every="24" cooldown="8m"></m-associate>
  </m-region>

  <!-- Efference: the HANDS. The conscious stream is never given tools; m-act reads a
       realizable intention and a realizer maps it to a wired capability. orient now
       names three channels — market, news, prices — so "let the headlines recede but
       keep watching the prices" is a reach the mind can actually make, and narrowing
       to one voice means following one coin. -->
  <m-act name="hands" every="8" threshold="0.62" cooldown="3m" intentCooldown="15m"
         prediction="on" compareDeadline="8s">
    <m-orient name="orient" cooldown="30s" intentThreshold="0.75"></m-orient>
    <m-look name="look"
            newsUrl="https://stereotic.com/data/news/token-specific.json"></m-look>
    <m-note name="note"></m-note>
    <m-recall name="recall"></m-recall>
  </m-act>

  <!-- Prediction notation: the private ledger (predictions/ledger.jsonl) plus the
       exact-fact store (facts/), both in the mind's own vault home. -->
  <m-expect-ledger name="ledger"></m-expect-ledger>

  <!-- THE MARKET-OUTCOME COMPARATOR — the thing this lab's header used to list as
       future work ("NOTHING yet settles a market prediction against reality").
       It holds the predictions it can extract unambiguously — one watched symbol,
       one direction, one explicit horizon — and when the horizon elapses it
       compares them against the live price and settles them: right, wrong, or
       honestly unsettleable. Being WRONG is felt at 0.92, louder than anything
       ambient; being right is felt at 0.35, quieter than a typical price move. A
       mind loudly congratulated for being right learns to predict safe things.

       DELIBERATELY OUTSIDE the market aperture. Inside it, closing the market to
       think would also close the mind's own accountability — it could shut its
       eyes exactly when a wrong prediction comes due.

       Read the component's header before trusting a settlement rate: on the one
       live run so far the ledger held a single `prediction` row and it was an
       expectation about an ACT, not about the market, so the extractor settles
       zero of it — correctly. Nothing in this architecture yet ASKS the mind for a
       market claim; a prediction producer is the next piece of work, and it is not
       this one. -->
  <m-market-outcome name="outcome"
                    watchTickers="BTC,ETH,SOL"
                    url="https://stereotic.com/data/stats/top100_stat.json"
                    maxHorizon="24h" pollCache="100s"></m-market-outcome>
  <m-facts name="facts" pinnedBudget="30000"></m-facts>

  <m-speech name="voice" every="6" threshold="0.6" cooldown="45s"></m-speech>

  <m-kb name="scribe" every="15"></m-kb>

  <m-economy name="economy" budget="1.00" estInPrice="0" estOutPrice="0"></m-economy>

  <m-console name="console"></m-console>
  <!-- port 7635 so a lab run does not collide with a public mind on 7627 -->
  <m-ws name="ws" port="7635"></m-ws>
</m-mind>
